Claim date: October 27, 2020

## Initial Q&A
### Did McDonald's Azerbaijan operate an official, verified social media account on any platform as of October 27, 2020?
Answer: Yes. According to the Daily Star article, McDonald's Azerbaijan operated an official, verified Instagram account with approximately 107,000 followers at the time. The article states: "The posts were shared on the verified account for the fast food giant in Azerbaijan, which has 107k Instagram followers." This confirms that a verified social media account existed as of October 2020, when the nationalistic posts about the Nagorno-Karabakh conflict were shared.

Source URL: https://www.dailystar.co.uk/news/world-news/mcdonalds-azerbaijan-promotes-war-armenia-22913718

### On or around October 27, 2020, did the official McDonald's Azerbaijan social media account publish any post, story, or advertisement related to the Nagorno-Karabakh conflict?
Answer: Yes, the search result confirms that the official McDonald's Azerbaijan franchisee published social media posts supporting military action in the Nagorno-Karabakh conflict. According to CTV News, "In a series of posts, the national branches of both McDonald's and Burger King shared their support for Azerbaijan forces in deadly fighting over the separatist territory of Nagorno-Karabakh." The posts were shared on both Twitter and Instagram during the conflict (which began September 27, 2020, and was ongoing at the time of the claim), though they were later deleted. McDonald's Corporation acknowledged the posts, stating, "Our franchisee in Azerbaijan has removed the social media content." This directly supports the claim that McDonald's Azerbaijan's official account was promoting the military taking of Nagorno-Karabakh around October 27, 2020.

